IMS Nanofabrication’s platform uses a programmable **512 × 512 array — 262,144 beamlets**. NuFlare’s advanced MBM systems similarly control roughly **260,000 beams**.

Instead of asking one beam to draw every shape sequentially, the system exposes a field with many individually controlled beamlets at once.

Think of one person coloring a giant grid square by square versus roughly 260,000 tiny pens working simultaneously.

The engineering problem shifts from “make one beam fast” to “control hundreds of thousands of beams with nearly identical dose, position and timing.”

## 4. Why complexity matters less to multi-beam

This is the central economic advantage.

With VSB, more complicated geometry usually means more shots and therefore more write time.

With multi-beam, the writer behaves more like a pixelated exposure system. The array scans across the mask while individual beamlets are turned on, off or dose-modulated.

The number of strange curves in the design does not increase write time in the same way.

IMS says its production multi-beam technology can keep mask write time below roughly **10 hours**, including for complex patterns. NuFlare likewise emphasizes high throughput that is far less dependent on data complexity.

That makes multi-beam especially valuable as computational lithography produces patterns that are better for wafer printing but worse for a sequential writer.

## 5. Parallel beams create new bottlenecks

Multi-beam does not make the problem easy.

The machine must solve several hard jobs simultaneously:

- keep hundreds of thousands of beamlets uniform;
- place exposures with nanometer-class accuracy;
- switch and dose-control individual beams at high speed;
- move the mask stage smoothly;
- feed enormous amounts of pattern data in real time;
- correct for electron scattering and resist effects.

NuFlare lists roughly **1.3 nm image-placement accuracy at 3σ** and about **0.65 nm local CD uniformity at 3σ** for its MBM-2000PLUS.

Those are mask-level specifications, not direct wafer dimensions. The point is the scale of control required before lithography even begins.

A systematic writer error can propagate:

mask-writing error → reticle error → repeated wafer imaging error → yield loss

## 6. Where the bottleneck actually appears

A mask writer usually does **not** sit in the daily wafer process loop.

Once a production reticle is qualified, a scanner can reuse it for many wafer exposures without returning to the writer.

So adding one more mask writer does not automatically increase wafer starts.

Mask-writing capacity matters when:

- a new tape-out needs a mask set;
- a node ramp requires many new masks;
- a design respin changes critical layers;
- a damaged mask must be replaced;
- computational patterns increase write demand.

That makes mask writing especially important to **development cycle time and mask-shop capacity**.

If the mask shop is constrained, an expensive fab can be ready while the reticles needed for the next design are still waiting upstream.

## 7. Why High-NA and curvilinear masks increase strategic value

As imaging gets harder, chipmakers increasingly use computation to shape the mask around scanner physics.

The best wafer image may require a reticle pattern that looks less like the original circuit layout and more like a mathematically optimized collection of curves and corrections.

That can explode VSB shot count.

Multi-beam is therefore not merely a faster writer. It helps make **computationally optimized mask shapes economically manufacturable**.

The second-order chain is:

better computational lithography → more complex mask data → more pressure on mask writing → greater value of parallel writing and high-speed data processing

A software improvement can create hardware demand upstream.

## 8. The supplier map

Two important advanced mask-writer suppliers are **IMS Nanofabrication** and **NuFlare Technology**.

